              text: "On a wet Thursday in Kentish Town, Dr Farida Haq did not begin with a ghost. She began with a number. In a [[peer-reviewed|revisado por pares]] paper from the Indian Institute of Science in Bengaluru, researchers asked fifty residents of a single block a simple question: when the landing lights flicker, what is the first explanation that comes to mind? Thirty-one people reached for something unexplained. Fourteen blamed the wiring. Five said they had not noticed the lights at all.<br/><br/>The wiring report, Haq pointed out, had been sitting on the hall table for six weeks. It is highly likely, she said, that most of the thirty-one had walked past it. \"This is not stupidity,\" she told a room of roughly forty. \"It is what brains do. A dull cause is harder work than a lively one.\"<br/><br/>Haq is a science communicator, not a [[sceptic|escéptica]] for hire. She has written about [[space exploration|exploración espacial]] for a magazine in Hyderabad and about [[superstition|superstición]] for a Sunday paper in London, and she treats both beats with the same method: take the claim seriously, then ask what would count as evidence against it. The Bengaluru study is small. She said so. A sample of fifty is not a city. The trend, however, is not a surprise to anyone who has worked in a [[laboratory|laboratorio]] and then gone home to a family argument about a noise in the night.<br/><br/>She offered a second figure, from a satellite-tracking desk in Surrey. Of the thousands of objects currently in low orbit, a handful re-enter every year in ways that look, from a kitchen window, like a sign. People report [[spacecraft|naves espaciales]] and angels with equal confidence. The objects are usually debris. The reports are not therefore foolish: they are what you get when a rare light meets a brain that prefers a story. \"In all likelihood,\" Haq said, \"your grandmother's omen and the Surrey desk are looking at the same metal.\"<br/><br/>A man in the second row asked whether she was telling people to stop believing things. She said she was not. She was asking them to grade the belief. It is obvious, she said, that some claims are bound to be wrong — a house cannot be empty and occupied at the same time — and that some are merely less likely than they look. Between those two heights there is a lot of room, and most of our evenings are spent there.<br/><br/>She walked through the scale she wanted the room to steal. Bound to. Highly likely. Likely. May well. Could. Much less likely. She wrote the six on the whiteboard and left them there. \"If you can put your claim on this line,\" she said, \"you have already done the scientific work. The rest is homework.\"<br/><br/>The last ten minutes were practical. If the lights flicker, read the wiring report. If a neighbour has a hypothesis, ask what would disprove it. If you have no data, say so: there is no conclusive evidence is a respectable sentence, and it is more honest than I think it's a ghost. On balance, she concluded, the unexplained is not going away, because the brain is not going away. What can change is the height at which we say it.<br/><br/>She was due to finish at eight. She overran by twelve minutes. Nobody moved, which is itself a finding, of a sort: a room of forty people is more likely to stay for a method than for a mystery, provided the method is told as a story. The whiteboard was still there when the caretaker switched off the lights. Bound to. Highly likely. Likely. May well. Could. Much less likely. Six heights, and not an I think among them.",
