              source: "Letter to the Kentish Town Gazette, signed Nora (adapted)",
              text: "Sir — I have lived on this street for forty-one years, and I am tired of being told that I have imagined the house opposite. I have not imagined it. I have watched it.<br/><br/>Nobody has had a key since March. That is not a rumour; it is a fact you can check with the agent, who is supposed to be selling it and who has not had a viewer in four months. The lights on the first floor still go on, most evenings, at a quarter past nine, and they go off at twenty to eleven. They are not streetlights. They are not a reflection from the bus. I have stood in my own dark kitchen and I have watched a lamp move from one room to the other. A lamp. Moving.<br/><br/>Last Tuesday a young woman from the library — polite, I will give her that — stood on my step and told me it was highly likely to be the wiring. She had a leaflet. The leaflet had a diagram. I am not a fool, and I can read a diagram. What the diagram cannot tell you is that the wiring report, which I have also read, is about the landing of our block, not about a house that has been empty since spring. She was mixing two buildings. It is the sort of mistake a person makes when they have already decided that old women see ghosts.<br/><br/>I do not say it is a ghost. I am not in the habit of using words I cannot stand behind. I say it is unexplained, and I say that unexplained is not the same as imaginary. The library talk, which I did not attend because I was watching the house, is supposed to have been very good. I am glad. It is meant to be a comfort, I suppose, to be told that brains prefer a story. Mine prefers a lamp that ought not to be on.<br/><br/>People in this street are more likely to laugh than to look. They were more likely to laugh in 1987, too, when the foxes first came up from the railway, and then the foxes were in everybody's garden and the laughing stopped. A thing can be unlikely and still be there. That is not superstition. That is patience.<br/><br/>I should add, before someone writes in to say I am confused, that I do know the difference between a flickering landing light and a lamp that travels. The landing lights in our block do flicker; they have done for a year, and the report on the hall table is about them. What I am describing is a yellow lamp behind a curtain, in a room that is meant to be empty, moving at a walking pace from the window to the back wall. It is bound to sound the same as a flicker if you have already decided that I cannot tell a lamp from a fault. I can.<br/><br/>The council is obliged to inspect empty properties twice a year. I have asked, twice, whether anyone has been in since March. I have not had an answer that would count as evidence. Search me, then, why a public body is more comfortable calling an old woman imaginative than sending a man with a key. I would rather be wrong in public than quiet in a kitchen.<br/><br/>I have no satellite and no laboratory. I have a window and a kettle and forty-one years. If Dr Haq would like a sample of one resident who has done the looking, she is welcome to my kitchen any evening after nine. I will not tell her what to think. I will pour the tea and I will point at the glass. The evidence, such as it is, will be on the other side of the road. Whether she calls it a phenomenon or a fault is her affair. I would only ask her not to call it nothing.<br/><br/>Yours faithfully,<br/>Nora",
