              title: "What Dr Google will not tell you",
              source: "Write-up of Dr Farida Haq's public-health evening at Kentish Town Library (adapted)",
              text: "On a Tuesday evening in Kentish Town Library, Dr Farida Haq did not begin with a virus. She began with a queue. Eighteen weeks is the target in England for a non-urgent [[referral to a specialist|volante al especialista]]. It is highly likely, she said, that many of the people in the room had already waited longer. The [[waiting list|lista de espera]] is not a rumour. It is a number the NHS publishes every month. What the number does not say is what people do while they wait.<br/><br/>They search. A cough that has lasted three weeks is typed into a bar before it is described to a [[GP|médico de cabecera]]. The first result is rarely a clinic. It is a page that lists twelve diseases, three of them grave, and a thread in which a stranger in another country is sure it is cancer. Haq called this [[self-diagnosis|autodiagnóstico]]. She did not laugh at it. «The search is what you do when a ten-minute appointment is three weeks away. The search is not the problem. Treating the search as a diagnosis is.»<br/><br/>She offered a second figure, from a [[public health|salud pública]] desk in Leeds. Adult [[vaccine|vacuna]] uptake for the autumn [[booster|dosis de recuerdo]] among people over sixty-five had fallen four points on the previous year. It is bound to fall further, she said, if the only message in circulation is that the jab is «just a cold now». A booster is not a rumour. It is a dose with a name, a batch and a date. People who skip it are not fools. They are more likely to have read a thread than a leaflet. [[Misinformation|la desinformación]] does not need to be a lie. It only needs to be louder than the leaflet.<br/><br/>A woman in the third row — later she gave her name as Nora, and she lives on Palmer Road — asked whether looking things up was being treated as a crime. Haq said it was not. It is obvious, she said, that a person with a new [[symptom|síntoma]] will look. What is much less likely to help is stopping at the first page. The evidence strongly suggests that people who bring a printout to the GP get a better conversation, not a worse one, provided they treat the printout as a question and not as a verdict.<br/><br/>She walked through the scale she wanted the room to steal. Bound to wait, if the referral is not urgent. Highly likely to be nothing serious, if the cough is new and you are otherwise well. May well be worth a booster, if you are over sixty-five or you work nights on a ward, as the nurse in the second row does. Much less likely to be the rare disease on page one. No conclusive evidence, if you have not been examined.<br/><br/>The last ten minutes were practical. If the wait is long, do not put off the appointment you already have. If a neighbour has a hypothesis from a forum, ask what would disprove it. If you have no data, say so. «I think it's serious» is a feeling. «There is no conclusive evidence yet» is a sentence a clinic can use.<br/><br/>She was due to finish at eight. She overran, again. Amy Wright, who had come off a late shift, stayed. Nora stayed. Valeria Soto, who had come because Amy had asked her to, stayed and wrote the six heights on the back of a leaflet. Bound to. Highly likely. Likely. May well. Could. Much less likely. Six ways of not saying I think, and a queue that will still be there in the morning.",
